    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue and positive feedback!\r\n\r\nI don't think it is *broken* per se. The ``(mini.ai) No textobject \"a`\" found...`` is not an error but a message indicating that `` a` `` textobject is not found. This message is there to indicate that failure to find might be due to configuration (small neighborhood or not suitable search method) and not text itself. See #135.\r\n\r\nHere is video of actually using it (with `cmdheight=1` and without 'noice.nvim'):\r\n\r\nhttps://user-images.githubusercontent.com/24854248/202900904-2c750f6d-e7bf-42d7-a71d-1ae000db474f.mp4\r\n\r\nThis message is shown because 'nvim-surround' relies on `a` textobjects to find surrounding. So for `q` alias, it seems to try `a'`, `a\"`, and `` a` `` consecutively. If some is not found, 'mini.ai' will show message.\r\n\r\nSorry, but I am not sure how to help here, to be honest. 'nvim-surround' heavily relies on usage of `a` textobject and I am not inclined to universally disable these messages. Here are possible options:\r\n- Open same issue in 'nvim-surround' in hope that it can be fixed on its side (for example, by using `` silent normal va` `` to select textobject).\r\n- Wait until #146 is implemented and disable all 'mini.ai' messages altogether.\r\n- Use [mini.surround](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/readmes/mini-surround.md) ;)",
